fa17f4e6f03d01cfdf9c0a86ebf42f1d" "basis" 1616 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14043400/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-31225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/e8f5f537ad63035cac39ea672d4fd2bfff61d97590f79f83277e6654c9c05893.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={h}^{″}(x),$ where the function $h(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $h(x)$ is concave up." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-31225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A function $h(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${h}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={h}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${h}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5).$ Therefore, the function $h(x)$ is concave up on the intervals $(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-31225" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9dad6845bf9eca2532d48ba52f667b28cf0c8fd38509d319edf3111cbe85818"} {"question" "q-31225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-31225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5)$"] ["text" "$(-3,0.75)$ only"] ["text" "$(-5,-3)$ and $(0.75,5)$"] ["text" "$(-5,-3)$ only"] ["text" "$(0.75,5)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-31225" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-3,0.75)$ and $(0.75,5)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-79041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=6{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-79041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12x-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12-12{x}^{2} \\\\  & =12(1-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12(1+x)(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(1+x)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-1) & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)<0$ when $-1<x<1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-1,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)>0,$ which has the solutions $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-79041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c9dad6845bf9eca2532d48ba52f667b28cf0c8fd38509d319edf3111cbe85818"} {"question" "q-79041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-79041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-79041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+6){e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({x}^{2}-2x+2){e}^{x}+(2x-2){e}^{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}>0$ and ${x}^{2}>0$ for all real values $x\\ne 0,$ the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$ So $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{2}{e}^{x}<0,$ which does not have solutions. So $f(x)$ is concave down nowhere.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fca91e185090cdf8e4aaa9a856e0e9f35f737ae1b73e24cbf042deb271228d1"} {"question" "q-114272" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha2